#
# ⛔ NOT REGENERATED IN CI. CI's Ubuntu ships FreeType 2.13.2, whose move arithmetic (the
# fv * 0x10000 / F_dot_P quotient), ODD/EVEN test, stack margin and DELTAP pop discipline differ
# from the 2.14.3 rekha targets; `fv_b1_regime` below is a move the two versions disagree on.
# Regenerate by hand on a 2.14.x host with
#   python3 scripts/hint_unit_vectors.py emit programs/hint_unit_vectors.cyr
# and review the diff like any other transcription. The emitter lints and formats its output
# before writing it, so this file is gate-clean by construction.
#
# Rules a consumer needs (each measured on FreeType, not read from a spec):
#   * hu_expect_x(p, i) is FreeType's REPORTED x of outline point i: the interpreter's cur.x MINUS
#     cur[pp1].x, because TT_Load_Glyph translates the hinted outline so that pp1 is the origin
#     (ref2143/ttgload.c:2578-2583); hu_expect_y is cur.y as it is. On font 0 lsb == xMin and no
#     program moves pp1, so the subtraction is of 0; `pp1_offset_readout`, `pp1_moved`,
#     `comp_noprog_touch_bits` and `comp_nested_first_point` are the rows where it is not.
#   * hu_expect_tag(p, i) is the tag byte: 1 on-curve, 8 touched in x, 16 touched in y, and on
#     point 0 ALSO 4 (FT_CURVE_TAG_HAS_SCANMODE) with scan_type << 5, which TT_Hint_Glyph sets
#     after any glyph program ran (ref2143/ttgload.c:839-840) — inside a composite, on the first
#     point of every component whose program ran. Mask with 0x19 (25) for the interpreter's
#     bits: 1 -> ONCURVE, 8 -> TOUCHX, 16 -> TOUCHY.
#   * hu_expect_advance(p) is slot->advance.x: pp2.x - pp1.x after the base layer's grid fit,
#     equal to ((cur[pp2].x - cur[pp1].x) + 32) & -64 (FT_PIX_ROUND's floor form), which is
#     cur[pp2].x - cur[pp1].x whenever both phantoms sit on the grid.
#   * hu_phantom_x/y(g, k) are pp1..pp4 of glyph g OF FONT 0 in FONT UNITS before scaling, per
#     tt_loader_set_pp (ref2143/ttgload.c:1336-1347) for a font with NO `vmtx` and NO `OS/2`:
#     pp1 = (xMin - lsb, 0) = (0, 0) because lsb == xMin, pp2 = (pp1.x + advanceWidth, 0), and
#     TT_Get_VMetrics (ttgload.c:112-138) gives tsb = hhea.ascender - yMax and ah = |ascender -
#     descender|, so pp3 = (0, yMax + tsb) = (0, ascender) and pp4 = (0, pp3.y - ah) =
#     (0, descender). The loader then scales all four and rounds pp1.x, pp2.x, pp3.y, pp4.y in
#     cur only, with FT_PIX_ROUND (`phantoms` reads back cur 0/448/576/-128 and org 450/600/-150
#     at 12 ppem; `phantom_pp4_floor` reads -160 -> -128; `phantom_vmtx` the vmtx branch). A
#     variant that changes an hmtx entry, the descender or adds a vmtx says so in its `differs:`
#     line; its phantoms follow the same rule from those values.
#   * a composite row (hu_program_gid(p) is 2 or 3): every component is glyph 1 running the
#     row's glyph-1 program with its OWN phantoms, then transformed by the record's 2x2 on the
#     hinted F26Dot6 points (FT_MulFix per term), translated by the scaled offset (FT_PIX_ROUND
#     when ROUND_XY_TO_GRID; scaled by the matrix first only under SCALED_COMPONENT_OFFSET — a
#     record with neither flag is UNSCALED on this host's build, `comp_scale_neither`) or by
#     the point-matching difference of hinted points (unrounded); with USE_MY_METRICS the
#     composite's phantoms are that component's, un-offset. A composite program (WE_HAVE_INSTR
#     with n_ins > 0) first clears every component point's touch bits, sets org = orus = cur
#     and x_scale = y_scale = 1.0, rounds the phantoms, and runs over the assembled zone
#     (ttgload.c:1178-1249, 777-860); without one (or with n_ins 0) nothing more happens: touch
#     bits survive and the phantoms stay unrounded. In a NESTED composite the inner glyph's
#     program runs on a sub-zone with first_point = its start: IUP and SHC subtract it, SHZ
#     does not (`comp_nested_first_point`, `comp_nested_shz_low`). A SIMPLE component's own
#     program always runs with first_point 0, whatever its base in the composite (every row's
#     hu_expect2/3 numbers say so: glyph 1's program runs at base 0, 14 and 28 there).
#   * hu_expect2_*(p) / hu_expect3_*(p) are FreeType's glyph 2 and glyph 3 of the row's font —
#     glyph 1 carrying the row's program, glyph 2 the row's composite program — loaded the
#     CONSUMER's way: one face, FT_Set_Pixel_Sizes once at the row's ppem, then FT_Load_Glyph
#     1, 2, 3 in that order, so `prep` ran once and the size's cvt, storage and twilight zone
#     carried from load to load as they do in rekha's context. Only the rows marked
#     `carries state` below differ from the same glyph loaded first on a fresh face (a twilight
#     point a DELTAP moved in the gid-1 load is moved again). points/contours/end/x/y/tag/
#     advance mean what the hu_expect_* ones mean; hu_expect2_points(p) = -1 would mark a glyph
#     that FT_LOAD_PEDANTIC refused in the sequence (none does in this file; the row comment
#     would carry the reason).
#   * a font's glyph 4, where `differs:` lists one, is a simple glyph that carries ITS OWN
#     program in the embedded copy (the only glyph that does: it is the font's, not a row's),
#     so a composite naming gid 1 and gid 4 runs two different component programs in one load
#     (`comp_wcvtp_shared_copy` and its siblings read back what glyph 1's program wrote), or
#     a 3-point glyph starts a nested composite (`comp_nested_shz_low`).
#   * twilight points are never in the outline; a program that needs one observed reads it out
#     with GC[0] on zone 0 and SCFS on a glyph point (the `read_twilight` idiom in the script).
#     Every twilight index used is below 16, the font's maxTwilightPoints and FreeType's own
#     per-run bound for this glyph.
#   * a program on font 1..38 runs AFTER that font's fpgm and prep ran (the prep at the program's
#     ppem); what its expected values show that font 0 would not is the carry-over of that
